# Project decisions (append-only; one line each)

- Renderer: GL Compatibility (rendering/renderer/rendering_method = "gl_compatibility") for the mobile-first build.
- Viewport: 1280x720 landscape (display/window/size/viewport_width/height) with handheld orientation sensor_landscape and stretch mode canvas_items.
- Scene entry: application/run/main_scene points at res://scenes/app/app_root.tscn (the AppRoot composition root, ADR-008 section 4).
- No autoloads in Phase 1 unless an ADR-008 section 6 lifecycle need appears; no match state may live in a global.
- Domain tile type is named TileDefinition (not TileData): Godot 4 has a native TileData class, so "class_name TileData" is a hard compile error.
- Score event ids are `score:<move-id>:<poker|edge|cluster>:<discriminator>`. Cluster events use ADR-007 section 5's documented `cluster:<color>:<1-based ordinal per colour>` form, and the canonical component key travels in the event evidence (ADR-002 section 7) rather than in the id.
- Tiles per player: each player holds a deck of six, dealt from the shared 144-tile pot at match setup (pot starts at 132) and topped back up to six when their turn ends, not after each placement (ADR-010).
- A turn is one or more placements by the active player, ended by an explicit End Turn action; each placement is its own atomic move. Move ids carry a placement index: `move:<match-id>:<turn-number>:<placement-index>` (ADR-010, superseding ADR-007 section 5).
- Deck size lives in a versioned deck rules resource (default six), never as a literal in code (ADR-010).
- End conditions are board full, or neither player holding a tile while the pot is empty. These replace ADR-003 section 5's two-consecutive-no-tile-skip rule (ADR-010).
- Turn flow: `MoveCommitter.commit()` performs the atomic board+score write (ADR-007 section 4 steps 1-7) and `TurnManager.resolve()` advances the turn (ADR-003 section 1 TURN_END, section 3 steps 8-11). Callers pair the two; nothing else may advance a turn.
- PlacementRejection extends ADR-006's recommended list with DUPLICATE_MOVE and MATCH_FINISHED, for ADR-007 section 5's idempotent-commit refusal and ADR-003 section 1's frozen-input refusal.
- Feedback channels (haptics and cue sound) are optional and independently switchable via the `feedback/haptics` and `feedback/audio` project settings, both defaulting to true; no rule, score, or turn path may read either, so a match plays identically with them off (ADR-008 section 5).
- Cue sounds are short one-shots under res://assets/audio/ mapped by FeedbackAdapter.CUE_SOUNDS, loaded once and played through an AudioStreamPlayer pool owned by the adapter (never an autoload).
- Feedback cues are fired by the screen when a human gesture resolves, never by subscribing to controller signals, because line_committed and score_awarded also fire for the rival's move.
- Edge colour is accessibility presentation: TilePainter.EDGE_COLORS is an Okabe-Ito palette and the single source of truth for edge colour, and the four top-edge overlays in assets/tiles/edges/ are tinted by it rather than drawn as authored. Colour stays the only edge channel by owner decision - a redundant pattern encoding was considered and declined, so this palette helps the red-green confusion pair but not every vision type.
- Phase 2 board readability: ClusterDisplayLayer draws persistent same-colour region perimeters from GameSessionController.current_edge_clusters() (which enumerates via EdgeClusterScorer.components_for_color), and sits beneath ScoreRevealLayer so the transient award reveal always paints over it.
- Rotation hints are a factual read-out only: GameSessionController.staged_edge_matches(cell) returns four booleans derived from EdgeMatchScorer, and the UI must never show predicted points or recommend a rotation, because that would invent a game rule the specification does not contain.
